From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 09:22:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Restore the immediate-update flag even if the forced tick
 throws

World.forceBlockUpdateTick set scheduledUpdatesAreImmediate, ran the block's
tick, and cleared it afterwards. Anything thrown in between left it set for the
rest of the server's life, and while it is set every scheduled update in every
world runs immediately and recursively instead of being queued - so all liquid
becomes synchronous cascades.

That is a latent bug in the original code, not one deferral introduced, but
deferral gave it a way to fire: the replayed forced tick reported earlier as a
crash on obsidian was thrown from inside exactly this window, and a live server
afterwards measured water block ticks at 1.42ms each against 0.005ms before.

Also tried and reverted: dropping the forced tick entirely and letting the
spring's own onPlace schedule the flow the ordinary way. It removes the cascade,
but no lava reaches water during generation and naturally generated obsidian
disappears completely - 544 blocks to none over 625 chunks.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/World.java b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/World.java
index 20093c8122985acbe0a326c5e9ec6bd94ee26cef..426e7c2366dd90605c00f18e83c1cbfd20adab7e 100644
--- a/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/World.java
+++ b/src/main/java/net/minecraft/server/World.java
@@ -2887,9 +2887,19 @@ public abstract class World implements IBlockAccess {
             this.decorationBuffer.recordUpdateTick(block, blockposition);
             return;
         }
+        // Athena - restore the flag even if the tick throws.
+        //
+        // It was set, the tick run, and the flag cleared afterwards. Anything thrown in between left
+        // it set for the rest of the server's life, and while it is set every scheduled update in
+        // every world runs immediately and recursively instead of being queued. One throw turns all
+        // liquid into synchronous cascades.
         this.scheduledUpdatesAreImmediate = true;
-        block.updateTick(this, blockposition, this.getType(blockposition), random);
-        this.scheduledUpdatesAreImmediate = false;
+
+        try {
+            block.updateTick(this, blockposition, this.getType(blockposition), random);
+        } finally {
+            this.scheduledUpdatesAreImmediate = false;
+        }
     }
 
     public boolean canBlockFreezeWater(BlockPosition blockposition) {
